We have the equation $c_1 \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} + c_2 \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}$.
This can be rewritten as the system of equations
\begin{align*}
2c_1 - 2c_2 &= -1, \\
3c_1 + 5c_2 &= 4.
\end{align*}
Multiplying the first equation by 3 and the second equation by 2, we get
\begin{align*}
6c_1 - 6c_2 &= -3, \\
6c_1 + 10c_2 &= 8.
\end{align*}
Subtracting these two equations, we eliminate $c_1$ and solve for $c_2$:
\begin{align*}
(6c_1 + 10c_2) - (6c_1 - 6c_2) &= 8 - (-3), \\
16c_2 &= 11, \\
c_2 &= \frac{11}{16}.
\end{align*}
Substituting this value back into the first equation, we can solve for $c_1$:
\begin{align*}
2c_1 - 2 \left( \frac{11}{16} \right) &= -1, \\
2c_1 - \frac{11}{8} &= -1, \\
2c_1 &= \frac{3}{8}, \\
c_1 &= \frac{3}{16}.
\end{align*}
Thus, the ordered pair $(c_1, c_2)$ is $\boxed{\left( \frac{3}{16}, \frac{11}{16} \right)}$.The answer is: (\frac{3}{16},\frac{11}{16})

